328 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 144 0 R
/E (Now, to explain the remaining boost settings.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
329 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 144 0 R
/E (GATE PRES: This is expressed in boost pressure. When the vehicle goes above 0vac, the B1 will hold the wastegate closed e.g. attempt to reduce lag as much as possible until the gate pres setting is reached, at which point it will switch to the gain setting \(explained shortly\). If your boost is set, for example, at 15psi, a typical gate setting to start with would be ~7psi. This should be set after duty.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
330 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 144 0 R
/E (GAIN: This is expressed as a percentage where 0% is minimum and 100% is maximum \(more aggressive\). The gains function is to reduce turbo lag. If this setting is too high, your boost may oscillate, be unsteady and/or spike when coming on boost. This should be set after gate pres. Increase this slowly e.g. increments of 5%, until your boost oscillates, gets unsteady or spikes when rolling on.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
331 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 144 0 R
/E (ANTIGAIN: This is like gain, but for when boost goes over the set pressure. Essentially, this is how quickly duty is decreased if boost goes over the set pressure. 0% means it will not decrease the duty over the boost pressure, whereas 100% means it will decrease duty a lot if it goes over the set pressure. A typical setting would be 10%; however, if your vehicle has boost creep or other mechanical issues, a higher setting may be in order.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
332 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 144 0 R
/E (If you adjust the duty cycle/boost, it may be best to revisit the 3 setting above to optimise the new setup. Also note these setting are independent to each memory setting e.g. it is NOT the same for all memories.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
